t way it can also  be played on smaller sets such as like  stereo speakers or mono speakers that's  two or one speakers respectively and so  when you have so much coming out of such  a little bit of sound what you often  have is like overlap which makes things  a lot harder to hear a little bit more  muddled because there's not many places  for that sound to go okay so we we've  got all this sound playing out at once  and I guess we have maybe more sounds in  our TV and movies than we maybe did a  few years ago or you know decades ago  but how are sound Engineers working in  TV approaching this  so the hard part about it is that based  on what I heard from The Sound Engineers  I spoke to is that they don't  necessarily have that much of a creative  say in the way that sound will  ultimately appear in the film or movie  that they're working on that's often up  to the director who will be like oh well  we actually want to make this sound even  Larger than Life so what often happens  is even though sound Engineers were  saying hey like there's all these  explosions happening the dialogue isn't  necessarily at the Forefront of this  director is like hey well if you were an  actual explosion you probably wouldn't  be able to hear the person next to you  anyway I mean it does often feel  difficult when you're watching you know  an action scene to hear what people are  saying in particular but you know we are  trying to to follow the story to hear  what's going on so is there a way that  people can improve their sound if they  want to be listening at home  yeah there are so many different ways or  different things that you can try it  doesn't necessarily guarantee that it'll  sound a lot better number one I guess  the most cheapest option that you can do  is just look at your setup if you have a  TV that has backward facing speakers and  you're hanging up on your wall then of  course that sounds gonna get a little  bit muffled so it might be easier for  you to either set t